## Why This Is Asked

Tests a new CKNE angle at the edge: cross-tenant cache resource partitioning with live reallocation under traffic bursts.

## Key Concepts

- CKNE→cache-size mapping
- Eviction aggressiveness tuning
- Per-tenant prefetch priority
- Minute-by-minute shard reallocation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-policy skeleton
function policyForTenant(ckne) {
  // map to allocation, eviction, prefetch
  const size = baseSize * (0.5 + ckne);
  const evict = Math.max(1, Math.floor(5 * (1 - ckne)));
  const prefetch = Math.round(10 * ckne);
  return {size, evict, prefetch};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How to handle CKNE drift and stability during sustained spikes?
- How to validate edge latency and hit-rate SLAs under churn?

## Why This Is Asked

Evaluates practical implementation of CKNE-driven throughput control at the API gateway layer, specifically assessing the ability to translate abstract CKNE values into concrete resource quotas and deadlines, and to adapt these allocations in real-time based on performance metrics.

## Key Concepts

- Dynamic CKNE-to-resource quota mapping
- Token bucket algorithm with CKNE-weighted refill rates
- Real-time rebalancing loop with SLA preservation
- Fairness constraints while prioritizing high-value tenants

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudocode: CKNE-driven resource rebalancing executed minute-by-minute
function rebalanceResources(tenants) {
  tenants.forEach(tenant => {
    // Scale token refill rate by CKNE value
    tenant.tokens += baseRefillRate * tenant.ckne;

    // Adjust upstream deadline inversely with CKNE
    tenant.deadline = baseDeadline / (tenant.ckne + 1);

    // Enforce maximum allocation caps
    tenant.tokens = Math.min(tenant.tokens, maxTokenCap);
  });
}
```

## Implementation Strategy

The system continuously monitors per-tenant performance metrics and reallocates resources toward higher-CKNE tenants while maintaining baseline p95 latency guarantees through a controlled feedback loop that preserves fairness across the tenant population.

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a scalable, fair CKNE-aware scheduler for a core data platform, with explicit controls for memory, parallelism, and preemption under mixed workloads.

## Key Concepts

- CKNE-to-resource mapping (parallelism, memory, spill budget)
- Minute-by-minute degradation loop and preemption
- Fairness guarantees and SLA preservation
- Telemetry for CKNE drift and resource usage

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo CKNE -> resource mapping
function mapCKNEToResources(ckne, base) {
  const maxConcurrent = Math.max(1, Math.round(ckne * 8));
  const spillBudget = base.spill * (1 - ckne);
  const memPerFragment = base.mem * (0.5 + 0.5 * ckne);
  return { maxConcurrent, spillBudget, memPerFragment };
}
```

